---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterHero from '../components/ChapterHero.astro';
import { pack } from '../data/pack.ts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Youth protection"
  description="A detailed look at the screening, training, and structural rules Scouting America requires to keep Cub Scout Pack 170 activities safe."
>
  <ChapterHero
    chapter="Read this before you decide"
    title="How Scouting America"
    accent="keeps kids safe"
    intro="This page is written for anyone deciding whether Pack 170 is safe for a child — a parent, a grandparent, a school or church contact. It goes deeper than reassurance: it names the specific rules, who enforces them, and where to verify them yourself."
    noteTitle="In a hurry?"
    noteBody="Report suspected abuse now"
    ><p slot="note">
      Call local law enforcement first. Then call the Scouts First Helpline, 24 hours a day: <a href="tel:18447268871">1-844-SCOUTS1</a>
      (1-844-726-8871).
    </p></ChapterHero
  >
  <section class="section">
    <article class="container paper-sheet policy">
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Adult screening and registration</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            No adult works with Pack 170's Cub Scouts by simply showing up. Every adult who wants a registered role submits an application,
            provides personal references, and undergoes a criminal background check run by a nationally recognized third party. The
            background check has to come back clear before the adult can serve, and it is not a one-time formality: Scouting America runs
            periodic rechecks of registered adults' criminal backgrounds to support ongoing safety, not just a check at sign-up.
          </p>
          <p>
            An adult application also has to be approved by people outside that adult's own say-so. The chartered organization — for Pack
            170, that is <a href={pack.charteredOrganization.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{pack.charteredOrganization.name}</a> — has to
            accept the application, and <a href={pack.council.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{pack.council.name}</a> processes the registration.
            An adult who has not cleared this screening, and whose background check and Safeguarding Youth Training are not current, is not registered
            — and registration is what Scouting America and the chartered organization use to decide who is allowed to serve with youth.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Youth Protection Training</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            Every registered adult — regardless of role, whether a den leader, a committee member, or an occasional driver — must complete
            youth protection training before serving with youth. As of 2025, Scouting America replaced the older Youth Protection Training
            (YPT) with an updated course called <strong>Safeguarding Youth Training</strong>. Adults who had already completed YPT kept
            their certification, but the training now has to be renewed <strong>every year</strong>, not every two years as it once was.
          </p>
          <p>
            The renewal requirement has teeth: if a registered adult's Safeguarding Youth Training is not current when their annual
            registration comes up for renewal, they are not re-registered. Lapsed training is not a paperwork gap the pack can quietly work
            around — it ends that adult's registered role until the training is retaken.
          </p>
          <p>
            This training is not locked behind registration. A parent who is not a registered leader can take it too, through <a
              href="https://my.scouting.org/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">my.scouting.org</a
            >. Scouting America's own guidance is direct about this: understanding the youth protection rules is described as every parent's
            responsibility, whether or not they hold a registered role.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Barriers to abuse</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            Scouting America calls its structural safety rules "barriers to abuse." They are specific, not aspirational, and they apply at
            every Pack 170 activity:
          </p>
          <ul class="policy__rules">
            <li>
              <strong>Two-deep leadership.</strong> At least two registered adult leaders, both 21 or older, are required at every Scouting activity,
              including meetings — not just campouts or trips.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>No one-on-one contact.</strong> An adult may not be alone with a youth who is not their own child, in any form — in person,
              online, on a video call, by phone, or by text.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Separate accommodations.</strong> No youth is permitted to sleep in the tent or quarters of an adult other than their own
              parent or guardian.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Appropriate attire and respected privacy.</strong> Appropriate attire is required at all activities, and adults and youth
              must respect each other's privacy, particularly when changing clothes or showering.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Buddy system.</strong> Youth use the buddy system so no one is isolated during an activity.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>No secrets.</strong> All aspects of the Scouting program are open to observation by parents and leaders. Scouting America
              does not recognize secret organizations as part of its program.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Digital and communication safety</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            These same rules extend online. Two-deep leadership applies to virtual meetings, not just in-person ones. The
            no-one-on-one-contact rule is explicit that it covers every form of digital contact — no private messages, no one-on-one texts,
            no one-on-one video calls between an adult and a youth. Any adult–youth communication has to include another authorized adult,
            and inappropriate use of smartphones, cameras, or other devices in situations where privacy is expected is prohibited.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Parents' rights</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            Every part of the program is open to a parent's observation, at any time, for any activity. A parent does not need permission or
            advance notice to ask what is happening at a meeting or event, and does not need a reason beyond wanting to know.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Reporting</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            Everyone involved in Scouting is required to report any good-faith suspicion of abuse to local law enforcement first — not to
            pack leadership, not to the council, and not internally instead of the authorities. Reporting to Scouting America is required in
            addition to, not instead of, contacting the authorities.
          </p>
          <p>
            Scouting America runs a 24-hour reporting line, the <strong>Scouts First Helpline</strong>, at <a href="tel:18447268871"
              >1-844-SCOUTS1</a
            > (1-844-726-8871), for known or suspected abuse or behavior that puts a youth at risk. There is no version of this policy that allows
            a leader discretion to stay quiet or handle a suspicion informally.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>What this looks like in Pack 170</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            Pack 170 has no members-only area — this website, like every part of the program, is fully public. Every contact channel on this
            site reaches multiple registered adults; the pack does not publish individual adult email addresses, so no channel here creates
            a private adult-to-youth or adult-to-family back door. Youth are identified on this site by first name and last initial only,
            and no photograph of a youth is published without a parent's consent.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pack 170 meets in space provided by its chartered organization, {pack.charteredOrganization.name}, in a building shared with
            other groups — not a private setting controlled by the pack.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Where to read the rules yourself</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>Do not take this page's word for it. The source material is public:</p>
          <ul class="policy__sources">
            <li>
              <a href="https://www.scouting.org/training/safeguarding-youth/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Scouting America — Safeguarding Youth Training</a
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.scouting.org/health-and-safety/safety-moments/scoutings-barriers-to-abuse/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener">Scouting America — Scouting's Barriers to Abuse</a
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://www.scouting.org/health-and-safety/gss/gss01/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Guide to Safe Scouting — Youth Protection and Adult Leadership</a
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://www.scouting.org/health-and-safety/safety-moments/scouts-first-helpline/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Scouting America — Scouts First Helpline</a
              >
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="policy__row">
        <h2>Questions?</h2>
        <div class="policy__body">
          <p>
            If anything on this page is unclear, or you want to talk through what youth protection looks like before your family gets
            involved, use the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>. It reaches multiple authorized adult volunteers, not one person.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  /* Each section is a row with its heading in the margin and its prose in a column of its
     own, so a long policy has a left edge to scan instead of one undifferentiated block.
     Values here are the ones accepted in live mode: narrow margin, snug rows, hairline
     rules. */
  .policy {
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    /* Hug the margin column, the gap, and the 68ch measure, so the sheet does not trail
       empty paper to the right of the reading column. */
    max-width: 64rem;
  }
  .policy__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 13rem minmax(0, 68ch);
    gap: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    padding: 1.9rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .policy__row:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .policy__row:last-child {
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }
  .policy__row h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--blue);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .policy__body p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .policy__body p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .policy__rules,
  .policy__sources {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
  }
  .policy__rules li,
  .policy__sources li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .policy__rules li:last-child,
  .policy__sources li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .policy__row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.85rem;
    }
  }
</style>
